Z||000014||SteveJ||09-18-2001||09:35 AM||||Anyone who has been in the military knows about following orders. Everyone has a part to play but you don't always know the whole plan. But you do your job, and the best you can. Then when it all comes off you see the plan in action with all the parts working.<BR>Right now we need to support our leaders and do our part. Don't expect to know it all. This isn't a movie.<BR>Control all your urges to talk about bombing innocents. Such talk is irresponsible. We want revenge but not the destruction of their innocent neighbors. <BR>Bush and other officials are telling you in so many words that they are going to do it right and it will take time to do. Patience is now the watchword.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Taijiguy||09-18-2001||10:28 AM||||In an effort to avoid outright criticism of Starfighter, I would suggest he do this: Go to MNSBC.com and read the hundreds of news articles there pertaining to all the various aspects of this conflict we are about to embark upon. It does no good to hurl bombs into Afghanistan and kill nothing but innocent (or not-so-innocent)civilians. Most of the terrorists are hiding in caves in the mountains and hills. There are political concerns to be taken into account, besides the fragile nature of our support from Pakistan, right now, we have the support of most of the world, but in some cases, that is a tedious thing, and we could piss alot of people off by proceeding carelessly. Without support from bordering countries, we would have a hell of a time getting troops, and equipment to the battle. <BR>Yes, it's a painful process, and the need for justice calls for immediate action. But knee-jerk reactions will be more damaging than constructive. If you watch the news enough, you realize that we are making a great deal of progress in tracking down the people responsible. <p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
